 with  structure  it's pretty straightforward it's divided  into these seven chapters each of which  are divided off by a little black page  and you know nice uh  what do you want to call this you know a  title  set of pages right and then it goes  right into the discussion you can see  that you know it's a hundred pages long  but you're not packing all that much on  each page  maybe 100 150 words tops i would guess  and so it's a quick read  there are these seven chapters it's  divided in in very sensible ways  the philosopher's doctor is kind of a  nice way to start we'll talk about that  a little bit more when we get to the  next section  what do you control plastic stoic  doctrine right the problem with emotions  dealing with adversity our place in  nature life and death  how we live together and then there's an  epilogue there's also a prologue to it  and there are some nice features to this  i think again for a beginner there is a  further reading section which is always  nice to have right and it's um  you know it's it's a decent there's also  the references as well  to  what texts are being talked about so the  further reading is about  four pages  and it includes you know what you'd  expect the works of the three roman  stoix it has suggestions for which  translations to get  along with you know the penguin great  ideas series  and there's there's the reference to the  complete works of lucius anea seneca  released by the university of chicago  press one references to  irvine donald robertson holliday and  hanselman  massimo pigliucci and then pierre ado  long and emily  wilson  um then there's john inwood and or  bradenwood and john sellers and there's  also a little thing here blurb about  stoic week and modern stoicism.com  which is a great place to go so you know  you've got a lot of stuff packed in here  the  style so sellers this is not his first  book he's written a lot of books and  john sellers  is one of the the rare people who can  write  really 