 that is  that's the last chapter in it there is  um you know a short epilogue  talking about um how the how the stoics  you know were read also in the middle  ages but then it talks about the present  and i'll just read you one more passage  since 2012 so this is written in 2019  over 20 000 people have taken part in a  global online experiment to see if  living like a stoic for a week might  improve their sense of well-being the  results suggest it does those who have  followed a month-long experiment saw  even greater  benefits he's talking about the stoic  mindfulness and resilience training  stuff so you know that's what this book  is basically talking about  you can get it and read it in the space  of an afternoon uh quite quickly maybe  even in the course of an hour if you're  already very familiar with the ideas  so that's some of the key points  so i think by this point you can tell  that i'm a fan of this little work what  other good points do i need to say about  it than those i've already highlighted  well i want to stress a point that i've  already made briefly namely that sellers  is very good at taking little bite-sized  chunks of ideas from seneca or epictetus  or marcus aurelius or some of the other  stoics that he talks about  and  bringing them together and he's chosen  them very well right so he's chosen the  really essential stuff and then he  brings them together  and forges them into coherent holes that  are more than the sum of their parts and  that's what's going on in a lot of this  i think the examples that he uses are  quite well chosen i think he's giving  just enough background  about these key stoic figures uh for  people to you know be interested for the  the history of these people to kind of  help you say oh i i get it but not to  overwhelm you as a matter of fact this  entire book is not overwhelming but it's  not underwhelming either it's just right  it's like a goldilocks thing so i'll say  that i think you know it's it's also got  kind of an attractive  look to it both with the cover and with  the  design of the book university of chicago  did a good job on this one  and you know i think it's well well  thought out well laid out so  the other thing i'll mention again is  that john sellers is very good at  writing  works that retain some academic  structure and rigor but are oriented  towards a popular audience i will be  giving this  to  my mother-in-law as a gift before too  long because i think she'll really enjoy  it and um  you know she's she's uh taking college  classes and actually earned several  degrees but i think this could be given  to just about anybody and they would  benefit highly from from reading it it's  not of course if you're studying  stoicism  it's a nice read  if you already know all this stuff  it's you know it's good  you're not going to learn  radically new things from it but i think  for somebody who hasn't been exposed to  it this would actually be a great  first step perhaps one of the best  is there anything that i would say is  problematic about this work there are no  errors at all which is kind of striking  because usually i can find something  where i'm like well i don't know about  that you know none in this book at all  um  so the things that i would say are  problematic are purely subjective and  one of them is going to strike you is  very very funny so you notice how big my  hands are and you know i can easily grip  this book  i actually find it kind of hard myself  because the book is so small  to  you know read around in it because my my  fingers are so big but  i'm on the large side of that sort of  thing that's probably not going to be an  issue for most people um it is designed  to be a pocketbook so it's got it's got  to fit a certain size right  um the price is you know  some people might say 12 is a little  high for this but  you know it's really well put together  um  it's nicely laid out it's aesthetically  pleasing it's it's it's a book that's  quite good so those would just be  quibbles that one could bring up i i  don't have anything else that's  problematic in any way about this this  book  all right so final thoughts a little bit  of wrap up um i  unreservedly recommend this book i think  it's a great little book  and i think it would be something that  would be best  circulating around as sort of an  introduction to stoicism as i mentioned  already  this was gifted to me i'm going to  continue that gifting by giving it to  somebody else who i think will benefit  from it who has an interest in stoicism  and this will hopefully spark more of it  and give her some solid structure and  then maybe she might pass it on to  somebody else which i think would  actually fit very well with some of the  thematics of this book so  i recommend it and  i think that if this is the sort of  thing that you want or even more so if  you know somebody who  would derive some benefit from it give  it to them as a gift  you

